-- | A plotting color: 8-bit red, green, and blue channels plus an alpha opacity, the C++ counterpart of R\'s @rgb(r, g, b, alpha, maxColorValue = 255)@.
--
-- Brace-initialize numeric channels: @dm::RGB{255, 0, 0}@ is opaque red and @dm::RGB{255, 0, 0, 0.5}@ is half-transparent red. A default-constructed RGB is the library\'s dark-slate ink @{31, 41, 55}@, fully opaque. Channels are not validated: keep them in \[0, 255\] (both the raster and SVG renderers clamp out-of-range channels), and keep alpha in \[0, 1\] (both renderers clamp it). Every plotting API takes RGBA (the same type as RGB) by value, and @dm::c(...)@ of several RGBs builds a palette usable with @select()@ and set\_col(). Color strings convert implicitly: names are case-insensitive black, white, red, green, blue, yellow, cyan, magenta, gray\/grey and transparent; hex accepts \#RRGGBB and \#RRGGBBAA, with alpha last and scaled to \[0, 1\]. Malformed text throws before a plot is changed. RGB and RGBA remain the same type, preserving existing palettes and the binary layout of numeric colors. Parsing owns no reference to the string.
