-- | Sets the resolution PNG and PDF output is rasterized at, like R\'s @png(res = )@ and ggsave()\'s @dpi =@ (default 96).
--
-- The layout, text and line widths keep their size relative to the plot; only the pixel count changes: a plot of width(units::inch{8}) saves as a 768-pixel-wide PNG at 96 dpi and a 2400-pixel-wide one at 300 dpi. A PNG records the resolution (its pHYs chunk) so other programs show it at its physical size; a PDF keeps its page size and gains detail. SVG output is vector and is unaffected.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > auto plt = dm::Plot(dm::c(1.0, 2.0), dm::c(1.0, 2.0)).width(dm::units::inch{2}).dpi(300);
-- > std::print("{} {}\n", plt.width(), plt.dpi());   // 192 300
--
-- __References__
--
-- * R png() @res@ argument: https:\/\/stat.ethz.ch\/R-manual\/R-devel\/library\/grDevices\/html\/png.html
-- * ggplot2 ggsave() @dpi@ argument: https:\/\/ggplot2.tidyverse.org\/reference\/ggsave.html
--
-- C++: @Plot & datamunge::graphics::Plot::dpi(double value)@
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.InvalidArgument') for C++ @std::invalid\_argument@: If @value@ is not finite and positive.
--
-- See also: @width(units::Length@, @height(units::Length@, @save@.
plotDpiValue
  :: IsPlot a
  => a  -- ^ the object
  -> P.Double  -- ^ Dots per inch; finite and positive.
  -> P.IO Plot  -- ^ This plot, for chaining.
plotDpiValue _self _x1 = R.call 485 [R.object _self, R.toCpp _x1] P.>>= R.result

