-- | Builds a frequency histogram of @data@ with @bins@ equal-width bins, like R\'s @hist(x, breaks = bins)@.
--
-- The bins split \[min, max\] of the data into equal widths (R instead treats @breaks@ as a suggestion and uses @pretty()@ boundaries). Bins include their left edge and exclude their right edge, except that the maximum goes in the final bin; R\'s default (@right = TRUE@) is the opposite, so a value on an inner boundary is counted one bin higher here. Constant data uses the range \[value, value + 1\]. Each bin is one bar of height equal to its count, centered on the bin with width equal to the bin width. The title is \"Histogram\", the x label \"x\", and the y label \"Frequency\". The data is consumed; the plot keeps only counts.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > auto plt = dm::Plot::hist({1.0, 2.0, 2.0, 3.0, 3.0, 3.0, 4.0}, 3);
-- > const auto& bars = plt.series()[0];  // bins [1,2) [2,3) [3,4]
-- > std::print("{} {} {}\n", bars.y[0], bars.y[1], bars.y[2]);  // 1 2 4
