-- | Adds the reference line y = intercept + slope \* x across the plotting area, like R\'s @abline(a = intercept, b = slope)@.
--
-- The line runs between the left and right edges of the visible x range and is drawn beneath every data layer. It does not change the axis ranges, and it is not clipped to the plotting area, so a steep line can extend into the margins. Stored as an ABLine.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > const auto x = dm::c(1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0);
-- > auto plt = dm::Plot(x, dm::c(2.1, 3.9, 6.2, 7.8))
-- >                .abline(0.0, 2.0, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38})  // y = 2x
-- >                .abline_h(5.0)
-- >                .abline_v(2.5, dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.0);
-- > std::print("{}\n", plt.reference_lines().size());  // 3
-- > plt.save("ablines.svg");
--
-- __References__
--
-- * R @abline()@: https:\/\/stat.ethz.ch\/R-manual\/R-devel\/library\/graphics\/html\/abline.html
--
-- __Warning:__ Only to\_svg() and @.svg@ saves draw reference lines. The raster outputs (@.png@, @.pdf@, and show()) currently omit them.
--
-- Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: @color@ = its C++ default, @stroke\_width@ = @1.0@.
--
-- C++: @RPlot & datamunge::graphics::RPlot::abline(double intercept, double slope, RGBA color = ..., double stroke\_width = 1.0)@
--
-- See also: @abline\_h@, @abline\_v@, @qqline@, 'AbLine'.
