-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- ConjugateGradient

-- | ConjugateGradient on the DifferentiableFunction callback interface. Nonlinear Polak-Ribiere-plus directions with negative beta truncated to zero, periodic\/descent restarts and an Armijo line search. It is not linear CG with exact line minimisation and does not imply termination in p steps. Returns when gradient\/change\/search conditions stop progress. The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent callbacks. Inputs are borrowed; coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if a callback throws. Calls use local optimizer state and propagate callback exceptions; sharing a mutable objective across threads requires external synchronisation. Normal return alone is not a uniform convergence certificate.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > const dm::optim::ConjugateGradient optimizer;
-- > std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 1000
--
-- __References__
--
-- * J. Nocedal and S. J. Wright (2006), Numerical Optimization, second edition, https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-0-387-40065-5. The implemented variant and stopping rules are specified above.
