-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- RbfGaussianProcessSurrogate

-- | Exact Gaussian-process regression surrogate with the squared-exponential kernel
--
-- @s\\exp(-\\lVert x-x\'\\rVert^2\/2\\ell^2)@.
--
-- fit() Cholesky-factors @K+\\sigma\_n^2I=LL^\\top@ once and solves for @\\alpha=(K+\\sigma\_n^2I)^{-1}(y-m)@; predict() then needs one forward substitution per point: mean @m+k\_\*^\\top\\alpha@ and variance @s-\\lVert L^{-1}k\_\*\\rVert^2@.
--
-- The two-argument constructor keeps the original fixed setup: zero prior mean, unit signal variance, no normalisation or fitting, expected improvement. The options constructor adds value normalisation, marginal-likelihood fitting of the hyperparameters and other acquisitions. The kernel is isotropic, so the inputs should be on comparable scales.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > dm::optim::RBFGaussianProcessSurrogate gp(1.0, 0.0);
-- > gp.fit({{0.0}, {2.0}}, {1.0, -1.0});
-- > const auto p = gp.predict({1.0});
-- > std::print("{:.4f} {:.4f}\n", p.mean, p.sd);  // 0.0000 0.5933
--
-- __References__
--
-- * C. E. Rasmussen and C. K. I. Williams (2006). /Gaussian Processes for Machine Learning/, chapters 2 and 5. MIT Press.
--
-- The C++ class @datamunge::optim::RBFGaussianProcessSurrogate@.
--
-- Derives from 'BayesianSurrogate': the functions of that class take its objects too.
