-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- Lbfgs

-- | LBFGS on the DifferentiableFunction callback interface. Limited-memory BFGS with weak-Wolfe bracket\/bisection search, descent fallback and positive-curvature pair retention. If the search budget ends, a previous Armijo step can be used; if none was found, returns. Tests gradient norm or absolute objective change. O(history\_size\*p) state; history\_size must be positive. The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent callbacks. Inputs are borrowed; coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if a callback throws. Calls use local optimizer state and propagate callback exceptions; sharing a mutable objective across threads requires external synchronisation. Normal return alone is not a uniform convergence certificate.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > const dm::optim::LBFGS optimizer;
-- > std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 500
--
-- __References__
--
-- * J. Nocedal and S. J. Wright (2006), Numerical Optimization, second edition, https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-0-387-40065-5. The implemented variant and stopping rules are specified above.
--
-- The C++ class @datamunge::optim::LBFGS@.
--
-- A value holds the C++ object; it is released when garbage collection reclaims the last value holding it, or by 'R.closeObject'.
newtype Lbfgs = Lbfgs' R.Handle

instance R.CppObject Lbfgs where
  objectHandle (Lbfgs' _h) = _h
  wrapHandle = Lbfgs'
  classIndex _ = 181
instance R.ToCpp Lbfgs where toCpp = R.object
instance R.FromCpp Lbfgs where fromCpp = R.fromObject

