-- | Run LBFGS from the supplied point using the configured finite budget. Limited-memory BFGS with weak-Wolfe bracket\/bisection search, descent fallback and positive-curvature pair retention. If the search budget ends, a previous Armijo step can be used; if none was found, returns. Tests gradient norm or absolute objective change. O(history\_size\*p) state; history\_size must be positive.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > dm::optim::QuadraticObjective f({2,8},{1,-2});
-- > dm::optim::LBFGSOptions controls;controls.max_iterations=5;
-- > std::vector<double> x{0,0};
-- > const auto value=dm::optim::LBFGS(controls).optimize(f,x);
-- > std::print("{}\n",std::isfinite(value)); // true
--
-- C++: @double datamunge::optim::LBFGS::optimize(DifferentiableFunction & function, std::vector\<double\> & coordinates) const@
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.NativeError') for C++ @std::exception@: Exceptions from supplied callbacks propagate. Raw solvers do not uniformly reject nonfinite inputs.
--
-- See also: @trace\_optimize@, 'LbfgsOptions'.
lbfgsOptimize
  :: (IsLbfgs a, IsDifferentiableFunction b)
  => a  -- ^ the object
  -> b  -- ^ Borrowed DifferentiableFunction with matching finite output shapes.
  -> IORef.IORef [P.Double]  -- ^ Owned-by-caller finite start; overwritten with the native returned point.; C++ writes it back
  -> P.IO P.Double  -- ^ Objective at the native returned coordinates; not a stop-status code.
lbfgsOptimize _self _x1 _x2 = do
  _v2 <- IORef.readIORef _x2
  _reply <- R.call 1929 [R.object _self, R.object _x1, R.slot 0 _v2]
  R.writeBack _reply 0 _x2
  R.result _reply

