-- | The definite integral of f (with every other name in f bound as usual by evaluate()) from a to b with respect to var, via adaptive Simpson\'s rule -- a numeric fallback\/ complement to Expr::integrate() for cases outside that method\'s supported symbolic forms (e.g. sin(x)\*cos(x), or any other integrand it throws on), or simply to check a symbolic result. Handles a \> b by returning the negated integral over \[b, a\].
--
-- Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: @tolerance@ = @1e-09@, @max\_depth@ = @50@.
--
-- C++: @double datamunge::algebra::definite\_integral(const Expr & f, const std::string & var, double a, double b, double tolerance = 1.0000000000000001E-9, int max\_depth = 50)@
definiteIntegral :: IsExpr a => a -> P.String -> P.Double -> P.Double -> P.IO P.Double
definiteIntegral _x1 _x2 _x3 _x4 = R.call 5 [R.object _x1, R.toCpp _x2, R.toCpp _x3, R.toCpp _x4] P.>>= R.result

-- | The definite integral of f (with every other name in f bound as usual by evaluate()) from a to b with respect to var, via adaptive Simpson\'s rule -- a numeric fallback\/ complement to Expr::integrate() for cases outside that method\'s supported symbolic forms (e.g. sin(x)\*cos(x), or any other integrand it throws on), or simply to check a symbolic result. Handles a \> b by returning the negated integral over \[b, a\].
--
-- Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: @max\_depth@ = @50@.
--
-- C++: @double datamunge::algebra::definite\_integral(const Expr & f, const std::string & var, double a, double b, double tolerance = 1.0000000000000001E-9, int max\_depth = 50)@
definiteIntegralWithTolerance
  :: IsExpr a
  => a
  -> P.String
  -> P.Double
  -> P.Double
  -> P.Double  -- ^ Target absolute error per the adaptive refinement\'s own error estimate; not a hard guarantee for a pathological (e.g. discontinuous or highly oscillatory) integrand.
  -> P.IO P.Double
definiteIntegralWithTolerance _x1 _x2 _x3 _x4 _x5 = R.call 5 [R.object _x1, R.toCpp _x2, R.toCpp _x3, R.toCpp _x4, R.toCpp _x5] P.>>= R.result

-- | The definite integral of f (with every other name in f bound as usual by evaluate()) from a to b with respect to var, via adaptive Simpson\'s rule -- a numeric fallback\/ complement to Expr::integrate() for cases outside that method\'s supported symbolic forms (e.g. sin(x)\*cos(x), or any other integrand it throws on), or simply to check a symbolic result. Handles a \> b by returning the negated integral over \[b, a\].
