-- | The cache root where downloaded datasets are stored.
--
-- The first of: @\$DATAMUNGE\_DATA\_DIR@; @\$XDG\_CACHE\_HOME\/datamunge\/datasets@; @\$HOME\/.cache\/datamunge\/datasets@; on Windows @%LOCALAPPDATA%\/datamunge\/datasets@; otherwise @build\/datasets@ relative to the working directory. Nothing is created.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > std::print("{}\n", dm::datasets::data_home());   // e.g. /home/me/.cache/datamunge/datasets
--
-- C++: @std::string datamunge::datasets::data\_home()@
--
-- See also: 'fetch'.
dataHome
  :: P.IO P.String  -- ^ The directory path (it may not exist yet).
dataHome = R.call 113 [] P.>>= R.result

-- | Sorted canonical names accepted by fetch(), including audited UCI tables.
--
-- UCI aliases are listed by datasets::uci\_catalog().
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > for (const auto& name : dm::datasets::downloadable()) std::print("{}\n", name);
--
-- C++: @std::vector\<std::string\> datamunge::datasets::downloadable()@
downloadable
  :: P.IO [P.String]  -- ^ An owned list of names, with no network or filesystem access.
downloadable = R.call 114 [] P.>>= R.result

-- | Makes sure a downloadable dataset is on disk and returns where it is.
--
-- For tabular datasets this is the CSV file. UCI names such as \"uci\_53\" and \"uci\_iris\" point to the same cache. UCI verifies size and SHA-256 on every access and stages complete bytes before publishing them. For the image datasets it is a directory: \"mnist\" and \"fashion\_mnist\" hold the four uncompressed IDX files (@train-images-idx3-ubyte@, ...); \"cifar10\" holds @data\_batch\_1..5.bin@ and @test\_batch.bin@; \"cifar100\" holds @train.bin@ and @test.bin@ (the binary versions); \"sudoku\" holds Norvig\'s @easy50.txt@, @top95.txt@ and @hardest.txt@. \"karate\_club\" holds the970-byte original ZIP, GML topology and source README; every cached archive\/member is checksum-verified. karate\_club() reads34nodes\/78edges. \"rebot\_devarm\" holds 38 pinned Seeed DevArm CAD\/URDF\/STL files and the CERN-OHL-W-2.0 source license in their upstream relative directory layout. DevArm verifies the size and SHA-256 of every cached file on every call; changed files throw without replacement. Missing files are staged and verified before publication. It uses the same offline\/mirror\/cache controls, and works without the optional native data-preparation dependencies. Hashing uses memory proportional to the largest source file (about 35 MB). Approximate download sizes: diamonds 2.7 MB, california\_housing 1.4 MB, MNIST 11 MB, Fashion-MNIST 30 MB, CIFAR-10 163 MB, CIFAR-100 161 MB, sudoku 14 KB, DevArm 56 MB.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > const auto housing = dm::datasets::read_csv(dm::datasets::fetch("california_housing"));
--
-- For a complete CAD asset tree, use @dm::datasets::fetch(\"rebot\_devarm\")@.
--
-- __References__
--
-- * Seeed reBot-DevArm source: https:\/\/github.com\/Seeed-Projects\/reBot-DevArm
--
-- Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: @directory@ = @\"\"@.
--
-- C++: @std::string datamunge::datasets::fetch(const std::string & name, const std::string & directory = \"\")@
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.InvalidArgument') for C++ @std::invalid\_argument@: for an unknown name.
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.NativeError') for C++ @std::runtime\_error@: if files are missing and downloading is disabled or fails, or a download does not match its checksum.
--
-- See also: 'dataHome', 'downloadable', 'downloadFile', @karate\_club@.
fetch
  :: P.String  -- ^ One of downloadable() or a registered UCI alias.
  -> P.IO P.String  -- ^ The local path of the CSV file or the dataset directory.
fetch _x1 = R.call 115 [R.toCpp _x1] P.>>= R.result

-- | Makes sure a downloadable dataset is on disk and returns where it is.
--
-- For tabular datasets this is the CSV file. UCI names such as \"uci\_53\" and \"uci\_iris\" point to the same cache. UCI verifies size and SHA-256 on every access and stages complete bytes before publishing them. For the image datasets it is a directory: \"mnist\" and \"fashion\_mnist\" hold the four uncompressed IDX files (@train-images-idx3-ubyte@, ...); \"cifar10\" holds @data\_batch\_1..5.bin@ and @test\_batch.bin@; \"cifar100\" holds @train.bin@ and @test.bin@ (the binary versions); \"sudoku\" holds Norvig\'s @easy50.txt@, @top95.txt@ and @hardest.txt@. \"karate\_club\" holds the970-byte original ZIP, GML topology and source README; every cached archive\/member is checksum-verified. karate\_club() reads34nodes\/78edges. \"rebot\_devarm\" holds 38 pinned Seeed DevArm CAD\/URDF\/STL files and the CERN-OHL-W-2.0 source license in their upstream relative directory layout. DevArm verifies the size and SHA-256 of every cached file on every call; changed files throw without replacement. Missing files are staged and verified before publication. It uses the same offline\/mirror\/cache controls, and works without the optional native data-preparation dependencies. Hashing uses memory proportional to the largest source file (about 35 MB). Approximate download sizes: diamonds 2.7 MB, california\_housing 1.4 MB, MNIST 11 MB, Fashion-MNIST 30 MB, CIFAR-10 163 MB, CIFAR-100 161 MB, sudoku 14 KB, DevArm 56 MB.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > const auto housing = dm::datasets::read_csv(dm::datasets::fetch("california_housing"));
--
-- For a complete CAD asset tree, use @dm::datasets::fetch(\"rebot\_devarm\")@.
--
-- __References__
--
-- * Seeed reBot-DevArm source: https:\/\/github.com\/Seeed-Projects\/reBot-DevArm
--
-- C++: @std::string datamunge::datasets::fetch(const std::string & name, const std::string & directory = \"\")@
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.InvalidArgument') for C++ @std::invalid\_argument@: for an unknown name.
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.NativeError') for C++ @std::runtime\_error@: if files are missing and downloading is disabled or fails, or a download does not match its checksum.
--
-- See also: 'dataHome', 'downloadable', 'downloadFile', @karate\_club@.
fetchWithDirectory
  :: P.String  -- ^ One of downloadable() or a registered UCI alias.
  -> P.String  -- ^ Where the files live; empty means @data\_home() + \"\/\" + name@. Files already present there are used as they are, except UCI, DevArm and karate\_club\'s verified files.
  -> P.IO P.String  -- ^ The local path of the CSV file or the dataset directory.
fetchWithDirectory _x1 _x2 = R.call 115 [R.toCpp _x1, R.toCpp _x2] P.>>= R.result

-- | Downloads @url@ to @destination@ with curl, unless the destination already exists.
--
-- The file is downloaded into a temporary directory next to @destination@, checked, and only then renamed into place. Parent directories are created as needed. @DATAMUNGE\_DATA\_OFFLINE@ is honored; @DATAMUNGE\_DATA\_MIRROR@ is not (fetch() applies it).
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > dm::datasets::download_file("https://example.org/data.csv", "/tmp/data.csv");
--
-- Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: @md5@ = @\"\"@.
--
-- C++: @void datamunge::datasets::download\_file(const std::string & url, const std::string & destination, const std::string & md5 = \"\")@
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.NativeError') for C++ @std::runtime\_error@: if downloading is disabled or fails, or the checksum differs.
downloadFile
  :: P.String  -- ^ An @https:\/\/@, @http:\/\/@ or @file:\/\/@ URL.
  -> P.String  -- ^ The final file path.
  -> P.IO ()
downloadFile _x1 _x2 = R.call 116 [R.toCpp _x1, R.toCpp _x2] P.>>= R.result

-- | Downloads @url@ to @destination@ with curl, unless the destination already exists.
--
-- The file is downloaded into a temporary directory next to @destination@, checked, and only then renamed into place. Parent directories are created as needed. @DATAMUNGE\_DATA\_OFFLINE@ is honored; @DATAMUNGE\_DATA\_MIRROR@ is not (fetch() applies it).
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > dm::datasets::download_file("https://example.org/data.csv", "/tmp/data.csv");
--
-- C++: @void datamunge::datasets::download\_file(const std::string & url, const std::string & destination, const std::string & md5 = \"\")@
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.NativeError') for C++ @std::runtime\_error@: if downloading is disabled or fails, or the checksum differs.
downloadFileWithMd5
  :: P.String  -- ^ An @https:\/\/@, @http:\/\/@ or @file:\/\/@ URL.
  -> P.String  -- ^ The final file path.
  -> P.String  -- ^ Expected lowercase hex MD5 of the downloaded bytes; empty skips the check.
  -> P.IO ()
downloadFileWithMd5 _x1 _x2 _x3 = R.call 116 [R.toCpp _x1, R.toCpp _x2, R.toCpp _x3] P.>>= R.result

-- | Builds a uniform mesh of @num\_elements@ equal-length elements on \[a, b\]. Throws std::invalid\_argument if num\_elements is 0 or b \<= a.
--
-- C++: @Mesh1D datamunge::fem::make\_uniform\_mesh1d(double a, double b, std::size\_t num\_elements)@
makeUniformMesh1d :: P.Double -> P.Double -> P.Int -> P.IO Mesh1D
makeUniformMesh1d _x1 _x2 _x3 = R.call 117 [R.toCpp _x1, R.toCpp _x2, R.toCpp _x3] P.>>= R.result

-- | Evaluate squared Euclidean distance using the original double arithmetic. No finite\/range validation. Differences, products and sum can overflow, underflow or lose low bits; NaNs propagate according to floating arithmetic.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > std::cout << dm::geometry::squared_distance({0,0},{3,4}); // 25
--
-- C++: @double datamunge::geometry::squared\_distance(const Point2D & a, const Point2D & b)@
--
-- See also: 'distance'.
squaredDistance
  :: (IsPoint2D a, IsPoint2D b)
  => a  -- ^ First point.
  -> b  -- ^ Second point.
  -> P.IO P.Double  -- ^ Rounded squared distance, possibly infinity\/NaN or underflowed zero.
squaredDistance _x1 _x2 = R.call 118 [R.object _x1, R.object _x2] P.>>= R.result

-- | Return sqrt(squared\_distance(a,b)) with its original range limitations. A finite mathematical distance may overflow through the squared intermediate; this convenience primitive performs no checked hypot or input validation.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > std::cout << dm::geometry::distance({0,0},{3,4}); // 5
--
-- C++: @double datamunge::geometry::distance(const Point2D & a, const Point2D & b)@
--
-- See also: 'squaredDistance'.
distance
  :: (IsPoint2D a, IsPoint2D b)
  => a  -- ^ First point.
  -> b  -- ^ Second point.
  -> P.IO P.Double  -- ^ Rounded square root of the double-valued squared distance.
distance _x1 _x2 = R.call 119 [R.object _x1, R.object _x2] P.>>= R.result

-- | Evaluate the original double determinant (b-a) cross (c-a). A positive exact determinant means counterclockwise, but this approximation can cancel, overflow or underflow and its returned sign is not certified. No tolerance or finite validation is applied. orientation\_sign() supplies an exact sign for finite stored binary64 points; this primitive remains unchanged.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > std::cout << dm::geometry::cross({0,0},{1,0},{0,1}); // 1
--
-- C++: @double datamunge::geometry::cross(const Point2D & a, const Point2D & b, const Point2D & c)@
--
-- See also: @orientation\_sign@.
cross
  :: (IsPoint2D a, IsPoint2D b, IsPoint2D c)
  => a  -- ^ First point.
  -> b  -- ^ Second point.
  -> c  -- ^ Third point.
  -> P.IO P.Double  -- ^ Rounded double determinant, possibly infinity\/NaN or a misleading zero.
cross _x1 _x2 _x3 = R.call 120 [R.object _x1, R.object _x2, R.object _x3] P.>>= R.result

-- | Construct a complete unconstrained Delaunay triangulation with exact signs. Retains the original one-vector signature and Triangle layout. Finite coordinate validation precedes even trivial results. Duplicates coalesce to earliest ids; collinear\/fewer-than-three distinct sites return no faces. Uses Bowyer-Watson cavity replacement with symbolic hull ghosts, avoiding any finite super-triangle whose proximity could remove valid narrow faces. All orientation\/incircle signs are exact for finite stored binary64, including subnormals\/extreme scales. Cocircular ties retain existing faces under the ordering in DelaunayTrace. Faces are strictly CCW, contain no ghosts and reference original ids. Finite output order is canonical as documented on DelaunayTrace. No boundary constraints, holes, Steiner refinement or minimum-angle guarantee is supplied. Input unchanged; O(n log(n+1)+u^2 log(u+1)) worst-case work for n records\/u distinct sites with ordered cavity maps and full live scans; O(n+u) working\/output storage, no shared state or optional resource caps. Exact-predicate cost is bounded per binary64 query.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > std::print("{}\n",dm::geometry::delaunay_triangulation({{0,0},{1,0},{.5,.000001}}).size()); // 1
--
-- __References__
--
-- * A. Bowyer (1981), Computing Dirichlet tessellations, Computer Journal 24(2),162–166, doi:10.1093\/comjnl\/24.2.162.
-- * D. F. Watson (1981), Computing the n-dimensional Delaunay tessellation with application to Voronoi polytopes, Computer Journal 24(2),167–172, doi:10.1093\/comjnl\/24.2.167. This variant uses symbolic boundary faces, exact decoded-dyadic signs, earliest duplicate identities and ordered cavity edges.
--
-- C++: @std::vector\<Triangle\> datamunge::geometry::delaunay\_triangulation(const std::vector\<Point2D\> & points)@
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.InvalidArgument') for C++ @std::invalid\_argument@: Any nonfinite coordinate, even on short input.
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.LengthError') for C++ @std::length\_error@: Counter arithmetic or container capacity exceeded.
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.NativeError') for C++ @std::logic\_error@: Internal cavity topology invariant fails; no partial mesh returned.
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.AllocationError') for C++ @std::bad\_alloc@: Allocation fails.
--
-- See also: 'delaunayTrace', @incircle\_sign@.
delaunayTriangulation
  :: [Point2D]  -- ^ Borrowed finite planar sites, possibly empty\/duplicate\/collinear.
  -> P.IO [Triangle]  -- ^ Owned complete finite mesh of the distinct sites\' convex hull, or no faces for collinearity.
delaunayTriangulation _x1 = R.call 121 [R.toCpp _x1] P.>>= R.result

-- | Capture actual symbolic-boundary cavity transitions from the same solver. No replay. Full finite validation precedes the original-record cap and all allocation. max\_tests bounds cumulative incircle plus hull-side calls, checked before each complete live-face scan. Trace entries charge one per insertion plus removed faces, boundary edges and added faces, including ghosts, checked before retaining the event. Points\/maps\/final output and temporary cavities are excluded from that entry count; caps do not bound bytes or wall time. Zero disables only an optional cap. O(sum cavity\/event sizes) additional history, at worst O(u^2), beyond the ordinary solver. Failure returns no partial result.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > const auto t=dm::geometry::delaunay_trace({{0,0},{4,0},{4,4},{0,4},{2,2}},5,10);
-- > std::print("{} {}\n",t.triangles.size(),t.work.incircle_tests+t.work.hull_side_tests); // 4 10
--
-- __References__
--
-- * Bowyer (1981), doi:10.1093\/comjnl\/24.2.162; Watson (1981), doi:10.1093\/comjnl\/24.2.167; records the symbolic-boundary variant rather than a geometric replay.
--
-- Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: @max\_points@ = @1000000@, @max\_tests@ = @10000000@, @max\_entries@ = @1000000@.
--
-- C++: @DelaunayTrace datamunge::geometry::delaunay\_trace(const std::vector\<Point2D\> & points, std::size\_t max\_points = 1000000, std::size\_t max\_tests = 10000000, std::size\_t max\_entries = 1000000)@
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.InvalidArgument') for C++ @std::invalid\_argument@: Any nonfinite coordinate.
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.LengthError') for C++ @std::length\_error@: Optional cap, count arithmetic or container capacity exceeded.
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.NativeError') for C++ @std::logic\_error@: Internal cavity invariant fails.
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.AllocationError') for C++ @std::bad\_alloc@: Allocation fails.
--
-- See also: 'DelaunayTrace', 'delaunayTriangulation'.
delaunayTrace
  :: [Point2D]  -- ^ Finite original coordinates, unchanged.
  -> P.IO DelaunayTrace  -- ^ Owned points, original-id maps, finite mesh, actual events and work.
delaunayTrace _x1 = R.call 122 [R.toCpp _x1] P.>>= R.result

-- | Capture actual symbolic-boundary cavity transitions from the same solver. No replay. Full finite validation precedes the original-record cap and all allocation. max\_tests bounds cumulative incircle plus hull-side calls, checked before each complete live-face scan. Trace entries charge one per insertion plus removed faces, boundary edges and added faces, including ghosts, checked before retaining the event. Points\/maps\/final output and temporary cavities are excluded from that entry count; caps do not bound bytes or wall time. Zero disables only an optional cap. O(sum cavity\/event sizes) additional history, at worst O(u^2), beyond the ordinary solver. Failure returns no partial result.
