dtwTraceEntries _x1 _x2 = R.call 134 [R.toCpp _x1, R.toCpp _x2] P.>>= R.result

-- | Compute unrestricted absolute-loss DTW and actual logical work without cost history. Owns the result; borrows finite inputs without mutation. No band, slope bound, step weighting, length normalization or fitted parameters. Finite ties choose diagonal, then advance\_a, then advance\_b. O(n/m) recurrence time, O(n/m+n+m) scratch\/output, with only two double cost rows and n\*m predecessor bytes. Complete input\/geometry validation precedes allocation. Positive overflowing off-path costs remain infinity; only a nonfinite final optimum is rejected. Under ordinary double arithmetic the returned witness sums to the returned distance in forward order. Concurrent independent calls share no mutable state.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- See DTWWork and DTWComputation for executed work examples.
--
-- __References__
--
-- * H. Sakoe and S. Chiba (1978), Dynamic programming algorithm optimization for spoken word recognition, IEEE TASSP26(1):43-49. doi:10.1109\/TASSP.1978.1163055. This is the unrestricted scalar absolute-loss variant, without the paper\'s window\/slope rules.
--
-- C++: @DTWComputation datamunge::geometry::dynamic\_time\_warping\_work(const std::vector\<double\> & a, const std::vector\<double\> & b)@
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.InvalidArgument') for C++ @std::invalid\_argument@: For empty or nonfinite input.
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.LengthError') for C++ @std::length\_error@: For geometry\/index\/container bounds.
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.ArithmeticError') for C++ @std::overflow\_error@: If the final minimum is not finite double.
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.AllocationError') for C++ @std::bad\_alloc@: If scratch or result storage cannot be allocated.
--
-- See also: 'dynamicTimeWarping', 'dynamicTimeWarpingTrace'.
dynamicTimeWarpingWork
  :: [P.Double]  -- ^ Nonempty finite scalar sequence, length at most INT\_MAX.
  -> [P.Double]  -- ^ Nonempty finite scalar sequence, length at most INT\_MAX.
  -> P.IO DtwComputation  -- ^ Independent optimal result and counts, with no retained input\/cost history.
dynamicTimeWarpingWork _x1 _x2 = R.call 135 [R.toCpp _x1, R.toCpp _x2] P.>>= R.result

-- | Preserve the original two-argument unrestricted absolute-loss DTW entry point. Same validation, ownership, deterministic ties, numerical limits and complexity as dynamic\_time\_warping\_work(); discards only its work counters. This distance is not a metric: unequal stretched sequences can have zero cost and the triangle inequality can fail. Equal-length diagonal alignment bounds it by rigid L1 loss.
