-- | Classify exact closed-segment contact and construct its unique point or overlap bounds. Evaluates four canonical exact orientation signs. Opposite nonzero signs on both baselines identify a proper crossing; zero signs use closed coordinate bounds. Collinear endpoint\/point-segment cases return Point; nonzero interval overlaps return Overlap with lexicographically ordered endpoint bounds. Proper crossing coordinates use a widened quotient of exactly accumulated determinants, then convex interpolation and final double narrowing. The contact decision is exact for represented finite coordinates; a constructed interior point is approximate, not a correctly rounded rational certificate. Trace and run use the same fixed-size producing record, with no replay or dynamic history. Constant bounded work\/storage, no shared state; borrowed inputs unchanged.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > auto t=dm::geometry::segment_intersection_run({0,0},{2,2},{0,2},{2,0});
-- > std::print("{} {} {}\n",int(t.contact),t.first.x,t.first.y); // 1 1 1
--
-- __References__
--
-- * Exact determinant motivation: Shewchuk (1997), https:\/\/www.cs.cmu.edu\/~quake\/robust.html ; standard closed-segment orientation and collinear bounding-box test.
--
-- C++: @SegmentIntersectionRun datamunge::geometry::segment\_intersection\_run(const Point2D & p, const Point2D & q, const Point2D & r, const Point2D & s)@
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.InvalidArgument') for C++ @std::invalid\_argument@: Nonfinite coordinate.
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.ArithmeticError') for C++ @std::overflow\_error@: A nonzero construction quotient or final point is outside the finite represented range.
--
-- See also: 'segmentsIntersect', @cross\_difference\_ratio@.
segmentIntersectionRun
  :: (IsPoint2D a, IsPoint2D b, IsPoint2D c, IsPoint2D d)
  => a  -- ^ First segment start.
