-- | Parses an adjustment method name, like R\'s p.adjust.methods.
--
-- Case-insensitive. Accepts R\'s names \"holm\", \"hochberg\", \"hommel\", \"bonferroni\", \"BH\", \"BY\", \"fdr\" (an alias of BH, as in R) and \"none\". Tukey\'s HSD is not a p-value adjustment (it needs the group means, sizes and pooled variance), so it is not accepted here.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > const bool holm = dm::stats::p_adjust_method("Holm") == dm::stats::PAdjustMethod::Holm;
-- > std::print("{}\n", holm);   // true
--
-- __References__
--
-- * https:\/\/stat.ethz.ch\/R-manual\/R-devel\/library\/stats\/html\/p.adjust.html
--
-- C++: @PAdjustMethod datamunge::stats::p\_adjust\_method(std::string\_view name)@
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.InvalidArgument') for C++ @std::invalid\_argument@: An unknown name; the message lists the accepted names.
--
-- See also: 'pAdjust'.
pAdjustMethod
  :: P.String  -- ^ Method name.
  -> P.IO PAdjustMethod  -- ^ The matching PAdjustMethod.
pAdjustMethod _x1 = R.call 191 [R.toCpp _x1] P.>>= R.result

-- | Westfall-Young single-step minP adjustment (Westfall & Young, 1993) -- a resampling- based FWER correction that accounts for the actual dependence structure between tests (unlike the analytic methods above, which either assume independence or make no assumption at all).
--
-- @resampled\_p@ is a B x m matrix: row b holds the m p-values recomputed on the b-th permutation or bootstrap resample (generated by the caller -- this function is purely the combinatorial engine, not the resampling scheme itself). @sorted\_p@ is the m original p-values sorted ascending, and column k of every row in @resampled\_p@ must correspond to the same hypothesis as sorted\_p\[k\] (i.e. the caller sorts once and reorders both consistently before calling).
--
-- Single-step: every hypothesis is compared against the same reference distribution -- the per-resample minimum p-value taken over /all/ m hypotheses -- so adjusted p\[k\] = (fraction of resamples whose overall minimum p-value \<= sorted\_p\[k\]). This is automatically monotonic in sorted\_p and valid under subset pivotality, but less powerful than the step-down refinement below since it never narrows the comparison set as hypotheses are resolved.
--
-- Returned in the same (ascending sorted\_p) order as the input; the caller is responsible for mapping back to original hypothesis order if needed.
--
-- C++: @std::vector\<double\> datamunge::stats::westfall\_young\_adjust(const std::vector\<std::vector\<double\>\> & resampled\_p, const std::vector\<double\> & sorted\_p)@
westfallYoungAdjust :: [[P.Double]] -> [P.Double] -> P.IO [P.Double]
westfallYoungAdjust _x1 _x2 = R.call 192 [R.toCpp _x1, R.toCpp _x2] P.>>= R.result

-- | Romano-Wolf step-down resampling adjustment (Romano & Wolf, 2005) -- like westfall\_young\_adjust, but more powerful: at each step k (in ascending sorted\_p order), the reference distribution is the per-resample minimum p-value taken only over the /remaining/ hypotheses k..m-1, narrowing by one hypothesis at each step rather than always using all m. This is the step-down generalization that controls FWER without requiring subset pivotality. Monotonicity (adjusted p\[k\] \>= adjusted p\[k-1\]) is enforced explicitly, since narrowing the comparison set at each step means it isn\'t automatic the way it is for the single-step procedure above.
--
-- Same input\/output conventions as westfall\_young\_adjust: @resampled\_p@ is a B x m matrix with column k aligned to sorted\_p\[k\], and the result is returned in the same sorted order.
--
-- C++: @std::vector\<double\> datamunge::stats::romano\_wolf\_adjust(const std::vector\<std::vector\<double\>\> & resampled\_p, const std::vector\<double\> & sorted\_p)@
romanoWolfAdjust :: [[P.Double]] -> [P.Double] -> P.IO [P.Double]
romanoWolfAdjust _x1 _x2 = R.call 193 [R.toCpp _x1, R.toCpp _x2] P.>>= R.result

-- | Classical equal-variance one-way ANOVA, like R oneway.test(var.equal=TRUE).
--
-- Tests equality of group means with between\/within mean-square ratio. Independent normal observations and a common within-group population variance give the exact F reference. This is not Welch ANOVA. O(N) work, O(1) auxiliary storage; input unchanged, no omission or shared mutable state.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > const auto a=dm::stats::one_way_anova({1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9},{3,3,3});
-- > std::print("{:.0f}\n",a.statistic); // 27
--
-- __References__
--
-- * https:\/\/stat.ethz.ch\/R-manual\/R-devel\/library\/stats\/html\/oneway.test.html
--
-- C++: @HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::one\_way\_anova(const std::vector\<double\> & values, const std::vector\<std::size\_t\> & group\_sizes)@
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.InvalidArgument') for C++ @std::invalid\_argument@: Invalid grouping\/nonfinite input, no residual df, zero within- group variation or arithmetic overflow.
--
-- See also: 'HypothesisTestResult'.
oneWayAnova
  :: [P.Double]  -- ^ Finite observations concatenated group by group.
  -> [P.Int]  -- ^ At least two positive sizes summing exactly to values.size(); N must exceed the group count.
  -> P.IO HypothesisTestResult  -- ^ statistic=F, parameter1=k-1, parameter2=N-k and upper-tail p\_value; no CI or group estimates.
oneWayAnova _x1 _x2 = R.call 194 [R.toCpp _x1, R.toCpp _x2] P.>>= R.result

-- | Welch\'s one-way test of equal means in independent normal groups with unequal variances.
--
-- Weights means by inverse estimated sampling variances and uses an approximate F reference. Inputs are unchanged. Work is linear in observations with O(k) storage for k groups.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > const auto fit = dm::stats::welch_anova({1, 2, 4, 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 3, 4, 5, 5}, {3, 5, 4});
-- > std::print("{:.4f}\n", fit.statistic); // 4.1238
--
-- __References__
--
-- * Welch (1951), On the Comparison of Several Mean Values, doi:10.2307\/2332579.
--
-- C++: @HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::welch\_anova(const std::vector\<double\> & values, const std::vector\<std::size\_t\> & group\_sizes)@
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.InvalidArgument') for C++ @std::invalid\_argument@: Invalid sizes, nonfinite observations, zero variance or overflow.
--
-- See also: 'oneWayAnova', 'gamesHowell'.
welchAnova
  :: [P.Double]  -- ^ Finite observations concatenated by group.
  -> [P.Int]  -- ^ At least two groups of size two or larger, each with positive variance.
  -> P.IO HypothesisTestResult  -- ^ F statistic, numerator and denominator degrees of freedom, and upper-tail p-value.
welchAnova _x1 _x2 = R.call 195 [R.toCpp _x1, R.toCpp _x2] P.>>= R.result

-- | Compares all independent normal group means using Games–Howell intervals and p-values.
--
-- Allows unequal group sizes and variances. Uses a pair-specific variance and degrees of freedom with a studentized range over all groups. Inputs are unchanged.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > const auto rows = dm::stats::games_howell({1, 2, 4, 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 3, 4, 5, 5}, {3, 5, 4});
-- > std::print("{:.5f}\n", rows.front().p_adjusted); // 0.07077
--
-- __References__
--
-- * Games and Howell (1976), Pairwise Multiple Comparison Procedures with Unequal N\'s and\/or Variances, doi:10.3102\/10769986001002113.
--
-- Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: @confidence\_level@ = @0.95@.
--
-- C++: @std::vector\<GamesHowellComparison\> datamunge::stats::games\_howell(const std::vector\<double\> & values, const std::vector\<std::size\_t\> & group\_sizes, double confidence\_level = 0.94999999999999996)@
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.InvalidArgument') for C++ @std::invalid\_argument@: Invalid inputs, numerical overflow or an unresolved critical value.
--
-- See also: 'welchAnova', @studentized\_range\_cdf@.
gamesHowell
  :: [P.Double]  -- ^ Finite observations concatenated by group.
  -> [P.Int]  -- ^ At least two groups of size two or larger, each with positive variance.
  -> P.IO [GamesHowellComparison]  -- ^ Owned comparisons in order (1,0), (2,0), (2,1), and so on.
gamesHowell _x1 _x2 = R.call 196 [R.toCpp _x1, R.toCpp _x2] P.>>= R.result

-- | Compares all independent normal group means using Games–Howell intervals and p-values.
--
-- Allows unequal group sizes and variances. Uses a pair-specific variance and degrees of freedom with a studentized range over all groups. Inputs are unchanged.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > const auto rows = dm::stats::games_howell({1, 2, 4, 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 3, 4, 5, 5}, {3, 5, 4});
-- > std::print("{:.5f}\n", rows.front().p_adjusted); // 0.07077
--
-- __References__
--
-- * Games and Howell (1976), Pairwise Multiple Comparison Procedures with Unequal N\'s and\/or Variances, doi:10.3102\/10769986001002113.
--
-- C++: @std::vector\<GamesHowellComparison\> datamunge::stats::games\_howell(const std::vector\<double\> & values, const std::vector\<std::size\_t\> & group\_sizes, double confidence\_level = 0.94999999999999996)@
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.InvalidArgument') for C++ @std::invalid\_argument@: Invalid inputs, numerical overflow or an unresolved critical value.
--
-- See also: 'welchAnova', @studentized\_range\_cdf@.
gamesHowellWithConfidenceLevel
  :: [P.Double]  -- ^ Finite observations concatenated by group.
  -> [P.Int]  -- ^ At least two groups of size two or larger, each with positive variance.
  -> P.Double  -- ^ Desired simultaneous coverage in (0,1), default 0.95.
  -> P.IO [GamesHowellComparison]  -- ^ Owned comparisons in order (1,0), (2,0), (2,1), and so on.
gamesHowellWithConfidenceLevel _x1 _x2 _x3 = R.call 196 [R.toCpp _x1, R.toCpp _x2, R.toCpp _x3] P.>>= R.result

-- | Tests group scale with Levene\'s mean or Brown–Forsythe\'s median centering.
--
-- Runs one-way ANOVA on absolute within-group deviations. Independent observations and comparable group distribution shapes support interpreting the result as a scale test.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > const auto fit = dm::stats::levene_test({1, 2, 4, 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 3, 4, 5, 5}, {3, 5, 4});
-- > std::print("{:.3f}\n", fit.statistic); // 3.550
--
-- __References__
--
-- * Brown and Forsythe (1974), Robust Tests for the Equality of Variances, doi:10.1080\/01621459.1974.10482955.
-- * https:\/\/docs.scipy.org\/doc\/scipy\/reference\/generated\/scipy.stats.levene.html
--
-- Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: @median\_center@ = @True@.
--
-- C++: @HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::levene\_test(const std::vector\<double\> & values, const std::vector\<std::size\_t\> & group\_sizes, bool median\_center = true)@
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.InvalidArgument') for C++ @std::invalid\_argument@: Invalid inputs, zero residual deviation variance or overflow.
--
-- See also: 'bartlettTest', 'flignerKilleenTest'.
leveneTest
  :: [P.Double]  -- ^ Finite observations concatenated by group.
  -> [P.Int]  -- ^ At least two groups of size two or larger partitioning values.
  -> P.IO HypothesisTestResult  -- ^ F statistic, two degrees of freedom and an approximate upper-tail p-value.
leveneTest _x1 _x2 = R.call 197 [R.toCpp _x1, R.toCpp _x2] P.>>= R.result

-- | Tests group scale with Levene\'s mean or Brown–Forsythe\'s median centering.
--
-- Runs one-way ANOVA on absolute within-group deviations. Independent observations and comparable group distribution shapes support interpreting the result as a scale test.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > const auto fit = dm::stats::levene_test({1, 2, 4, 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 3, 4, 5, 5}, {3, 5, 4});
-- > std::print("{:.3f}\n", fit.statistic); // 3.550
--
-- __References__
--
-- * Brown and Forsythe (1974), Robust Tests for the Equality of Variances, doi:10.1080\/01621459.1974.10482955.
-- * https:\/\/docs.scipy.org\/doc\/scipy\/reference\/generated\/scipy.stats.levene.html
--
-- C++: @HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::levene\_test(const std::vector\<double\> & values, const std::vector\<std::size\_t\> & group\_sizes, bool median\_center = true)@
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.InvalidArgument') for C++ @std::invalid\_argument@: Invalid inputs, zero residual deviation variance or overflow.
--
-- See also: 'bartlettTest', 'flignerKilleenTest'.
leveneTestWithMedianCenter
  :: [P.Double]  -- ^ Finite observations concatenated by group.
  -> [P.Int]  -- ^ At least two groups of size two or larger partitioning values.
  -> P.Bool  -- ^ Use group medians when true (default), or group means when false.
  -> P.IO HypothesisTestResult  -- ^ F statistic, two degrees of freedom and an approximate upper-tail p-value.
leveneTestWithMedianCenter _x1 _x2 _x3 = R.call 197 [R.toCpp _x1, R.toCpp _x2, R.toCpp _x3] P.>>= R.result

-- | Bartlett\'s test of equal variances for independent normal groups.
--
-- Compares the pooled log variance with the weighted group log variances and applies Bartlett\'s finite-sample correction. Inputs are unchanged.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > const auto fit = dm::stats::bartlett_test({1, 2, 4, 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 3, 4, 5, 5}, {3, 5, 4});
-- > std::print("{:.3f}\n", fit.statistic); // 6.496
--
-- __References__
--
-- * https:\/\/stat.ethz.ch\/R-manual\/R-devel\/library\/stats\/html\/bartlett.test.html
--
-- C++: @HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::bartlett\_test(const std::vector\<double\> & values, const std::vector\<std::size\_t\> & group\_sizes)@
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.InvalidArgument') for C++ @std::invalid\_argument@: Invalid inputs, zero group variance or overflow.
--
-- See also: 'leveneTest', 'flignerKilleenTest'.
bartlettTest
  :: [P.Double]  -- ^ Finite observations concatenated by group.
  -> [P.Int]  -- ^ At least two groups of size two or larger, each with positive variance.
  -> P.IO HypothesisTestResult  -- ^ K-squared statistic, k-1 degrees of freedom and approximate chi-square upper tail.
bartlettTest _x1 _x2 = R.call 198 [R.toCpp _x1, R.toCpp _x2] P.>>= R.result

-- | Fligner–Killeen scale test using normal scores of median-centered absolute deviations.
--
-- Ranks the pooled deviations with midranks for ties. The chi-square reference assumes independent groups and is asymptotic.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > const auto fit = dm::stats::fligner_killeen_test({1, 2, 4, 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 3, 4, 5, 5}, {3, 5, 4});
-- > std::print("{:.3f}\n", fit.statistic); // 4.128
--
-- __References__
--
-- * https:\/\/stat.ethz.ch\/R-manual\/R-devel\/library\/stats\/html\/fligner.test.html
--
-- C++: @HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::fligner\_killeen\_test(const std::vector\<double\> & values, const std::vector\<std::size\_t\> & group\_sizes)@
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.InvalidArgument') for C++ @std::invalid\_argument@: Invalid inputs, zero variation in scores or overflow.
--
-- See also: 'leveneTest', 'bartlettTest'.
flignerKilleenTest
  :: [P.Double]  -- ^ Finite observations concatenated by group.
  -> [P.Int]  -- ^ At least two groups of size two or larger partitioning values.
  -> P.IO HypothesisTestResult  -- ^ Chi-square statistic, k-1 degrees of freedom and the approximate upper-tail p-value.
flignerKilleenTest _x1 _x2 = R.call 199 [R.toCpp _x1, R.toCpp _x2] P.>>= R.result

-- | Tie-corrected Kruskal-Wallis rank test, like R kruskal.test().
--
-- Ranks the pooled observations with average ties and uses the chi-square(k-1) approximation. The usual exchangeability null is identical independent group distributions; a common-shape shift model permits a location interpretation. It is not an omnibus test for every possible distributional difference. Small groups can invalidate the approximation. O(N log N) work\/O(N) space, no mutation, omission or shared state.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > const auto h=dm::stats::kruskal_wallis_test({1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9},{3,3,3});
-- > std::print("{:.1f}\n",h.statistic); // 7.2
--
-- __References__
--
-- * https:\/\/stat.ethz.ch\/R-manual\/R-devel\/library\/stats\/html\/kruskal.test.html
--
-- C++: @HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::kruskal\_wallis\_test(const std::vector\<double\> & values, const std::vector\<std::size\_t\> & group\_sizes)@
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.InvalidArgument') for C++ @std::invalid\_argument@: Invalid groups, nonfinite input or all observations tied.
--
-- See also: 'HypothesisTestResult'.
kruskalWallisTest
  :: [P.Double]  -- ^ Finite observations concatenated by group.
  -> [P.Int]  -- ^ At least two positive group sizes summing to values.size().
  -> P.IO HypothesisTestResult  -- ^ statistic=H, parameter1=k-1 and upper-tail approximate p\_value; no CI.
kruskalWallisTest _x1 _x2 = R.call 200 [R.toCpp _x1, R.toCpp _x2] P.>>= R.result

-- | Performs Dunn pairwise comparisons on ranks pooled across all groups.
--
-- Tied observations get average ranks, and their reduced pooled rank variance enters the normal approximation. The standard interpretation assumes independent groups. A location interpretation uses a common-shape shift model.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > const auto rows = dm::stats::dunn_test({1, 2, 4, 5, 7, 8}, {2, 2, 2});
-- > std::print("{}\n", rows.size()); // 3
--
-- __References__
--
-- * Dunn (1964), Multiple Comparisons Using Rank Sums, doi:10.2307\/1266041.
-- * https:\/\/cran.r-project.org\/web\/packages\/dunn.test\/index.html
--
-- Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: @adjustment@ = @PAdjustMethodHolm@.
--
-- C++: @std::vector\<DunnComparison\> datamunge::stats::dunn\_test(const std::vector\<double\> & values, const std::vector\<std::size\_t\> & group\_sizes, PAdjustMethod adjustment = Holm)@
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.InvalidArgument') for C++ @std::invalid\_argument@: Invalid groups or zero pooled rank variation.
--
-- See also: 'kruskalWallisTest', 'pAdjust'.
dunnTest
  :: [P.Double]  -- ^ Finite observations, concatenated by group.
  -> [P.Int]  -- ^ At least two positive counts that partition values.
  -> P.IO [DunnComparison]  -- ^ Owned comparisons in order (1,0), (2,0), (2,1), and so on.
dunnTest _x1 _x2 = R.call 201 [R.toCpp _x1, R.toCpp _x2] P.>>= R.result

-- | Performs Dunn pairwise comparisons on ranks pooled across all groups.
--
-- Tied observations get average ranks, and their reduced pooled rank variance enters the normal approximation. The standard interpretation assumes independent groups. A location interpretation uses a common-shape shift model.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > const auto rows = dm::stats::dunn_test({1, 2, 4, 5, 7, 8}, {2, 2, 2});
-- > std::print("{}\n", rows.size()); // 3
--
-- __References__
--
-- * Dunn (1964), Multiple Comparisons Using Rank Sums, doi:10.2307\/1266041.
-- * https:\/\/cran.r-project.org\/web\/packages\/dunn.test\/index.html
--
-- C++: @std::vector\<DunnComparison\> datamunge::stats::dunn\_test(const std::vector\<double\> & values, const std::vector\<std::size\_t\> & group\_sizes, PAdjustMethod adjustment = Holm)@
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.InvalidArgument') for C++ @std::invalid\_argument@: Invalid groups or zero pooled rank variation.
--
-- See also: 'kruskalWallisTest', 'pAdjust'.
dunnTestWithAdjustment
  :: [P.Double]  -- ^ Finite observations, concatenated by group.
  -> [P.Int]  -- ^ At least two positive counts that partition values.
  -> PAdjustMethod  -- ^ Multiplicity method for the whole pairwise family, Holm by default.
  -> P.IO [DunnComparison]  -- ^ Owned comparisons in order (1,0), (2,0), (2,1), and so on.
dunnTestWithAdjustment _x1 _x2 _x3 = R.call 201 [R.toCpp _x1, R.toCpp _x2, R.toCpp _x3] P.>>= R.result

-- | Pearson chi-square test of specified category probabilities, like R chisq.test().
--
-- Uses sum((observed-expected)^2\/expected) and chi-square(k-1); the probabilities are fixed, not estimated here. Expected counts must be sufficiently large for the approximation. Nonnegative fractional counts are accepted algebraically but do not automatically define a sampling model. O(k) work\/storage, inputs unchanged, no shared state or omission.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > const auto q=dm::stats::chi_squared_goodness_of_fit({10,10});
-- > std::print("{:.0f}\n",q.statistic); // 0
--
-- __References__
--
-- * https:\/\/stat.ethz.ch\/R-manual\/R-devel\/library\/stats\/html\/chisq.test.html
--
-- Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: @expected\_probabilities@ = its C++ default.
--
-- C++: @HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::chi\_squared\_goodness\_of\_fit(const std::vector\<double\> & observed, const std::vector\<double\> & expected\_probabilities = {})@
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.InvalidArgument') for C++ @std::invalid\_argument@: Invalid counts\/probabilities, sizes or zero\/nonfinite total.
--
-- See also: 'HypothesisTestResult'.
chiSquaredGoodnessOfFit
  :: [P.Double]  -- ^ At least two finite nonnegative counts with positive finite total.
  -> P.IO HypothesisTestResult  -- ^ statistic=X-squared, parameter1=k-1 and approximate upper-tail p\_value; no CI.
chiSquaredGoodnessOfFit _x1 = R.call 202 [R.toCpp _x1] P.>>= R.result

-- | Pearson chi-square test of specified category probabilities, like R chisq.test().
--
-- Uses sum((observed-expected)^2\/expected) and chi-square(k-1); the probabilities are fixed, not estimated here. Expected counts must be sufficiently large for the approximation. Nonnegative fractional counts are accepted algebraically but do not automatically define a sampling model. O(k) work\/storage, inputs unchanged, no shared state or omission.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > const auto q=dm::stats::chi_squared_goodness_of_fit({10,10});
-- > std::print("{:.0f}\n",q.statistic); // 0
--
-- __References__
--
-- * https:\/\/stat.ethz.ch\/R-manual\/R-devel\/library\/stats\/html\/chisq.test.html
--
-- C++: @HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::chi\_squared\_goodness\_of\_fit(const std::vector\<double\> & observed, const std::vector\<double\> & expected\_probabilities = {})@
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.InvalidArgument') for C++ @std::invalid\_argument@: Invalid counts\/probabilities, sizes or zero\/nonfinite total.
--
-- See also: 'HypothesisTestResult'.
chiSquaredGoodnessOfFitWithExpectedProbabilities
  :: [P.Double]  -- ^ At least two finite nonnegative counts with positive finite total.
  -> [P.Double]  -- ^ Empty (default) means uniform; otherwise positive finite probabilities matching counts and summing to one within 1e-10.
  -> P.IO HypothesisTestResult  -- ^ statistic=X-squared, parameter1=k-1 and approximate upper-tail p\_value; no CI.
chiSquaredGoodnessOfFitWithExpectedProbabilities _x1 _x2 = R.call 202 [R.toCpp _x1, R.toCpp _x2] P.>>= R.result

-- | Cressie–Read power-divergence goodness-of-fit test for specified probabilities.
--
-- Power 1 gives Pearson\'s statistic, 0 the likelihood-ratio G statistic, and -1 its reverse. A zero observed count is treated by continuity and yields an infinite divergence when power is at most -1. Uses an asymptotic chi-square reference.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > const auto fit = dm::stats::power_divergence_test({25, 15}, {.5, .5}, 1);
-- > std::print("{:.1f}\n", fit.statistic); // 2.5
--
-- __References__
--
-- * https:\/\/docs.scipy.org\/doc\/scipy\/reference\/generated\/scipy.stats.power\_divergence.html
--
-- Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: @expected\_probabilities@ = its C++ default, @power@ = @0.0@, @estimated\_parameters@ = @0@.
--
-- C++: @HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::power\_divergence\_test(const std::vector\<double\> & observed, const std::vector\<double\> & expected\_probabilities = {}, double power = 0.0, std::size\_t estimated\_parameters = 0)@
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.InvalidArgument') for C++ @std::invalid\_argument@: Invalid counts, probabilities, power or degrees of freedom.
--
-- See also: 'chiSquaredGoodnessOfFit'.
powerDivergenceTest
  :: [P.Double]  -- ^ At least two finite nonnegative counts with positive finite total.
  -> P.IO HypothesisTestResult  -- ^ Divergence statistic, degrees of freedom and upper-tail p-value.
powerDivergenceTest _x1 = R.call 203 [R.toCpp _x1] P.>>= R.result

-- | Cressie–Read power-divergence goodness-of-fit test for specified probabilities.
--
-- Power 1 gives Pearson\'s statistic, 0 the likelihood-ratio G statistic, and -1 its reverse. A zero observed count is treated by continuity and yields an infinite divergence when power is at most -1. Uses an asymptotic chi-square reference.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > const auto fit = dm::stats::power_divergence_test({25, 15}, {.5, .5}, 1);
-- > std::print("{:.1f}\n", fit.statistic); // 2.5
--
-- __References__
--
-- * https:\/\/docs.scipy.org\/doc\/scipy\/reference\/generated\/scipy.stats.power\_divergence.html
--
-- Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: @power@ = @0.0@, @estimated\_parameters@ = @0@.
