-- | One-sample KS test against a fully specified continuous normal CDF.
--
-- Two-sided p-values use the Stephens corrected asymptotic Kolmogorov reference; one-sided values use exp(-2/n/D^2). Mean and sd must be fixed independently of this sample: fitting them here invalidates the stated calibration. This is not a Lilliefors test. Ties are accepted, but continuous-null calibration is affected by rounding. O(n log n) work\/O(n) storage, input unchanged, no omission\/shared state.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > const auto k=dm::stats::ks_test_one_sample_normal({-1,0,1});
-- > std::print("{:.0f}\n",k.parameter1); // 3
--
-- __References__
--
-- * https:\/\/stat.ethz.ch\/R-manual\/R-devel\/library\/stats\/html\/ks.test.html
--
-- Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: @mean@ = @0.0@, @sd@ = @1.0@, @alternative@ = @AlternativeTwoSided@.
--
-- C++: @HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::ks\_test\_one\_sample\_normal(const std::vector\<double\> & x, double mean = 0.0, double sd = 1.0, Alternative alternative = TwoSided)@
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.InvalidArgument') for C++ @std::invalid\_argument@: Too few\/nonfinite values, invalid mean\/sd or alternative.
--
-- See also: 'HypothesisTestResult'.
