tTestPairedWithConfLevel _x1 _x2 _x3 _x4 = R.call 224 [R.toCpp _x1, R.toCpp _x2, R.toCpp _x3, R.toCpp _x4] P.>>= R.result

-- | F-test and interval for a ratio of variances, like R var.test().
--
-- Uses sample variance(x)\/sample variance(y). Independent normal populations give the F(df\_x,df\_y) reference; it is sensitive to nonnormality and is not a gatekeeper for choosing Welch versus pooled t tests. O(n\_x+n\_y) work\/O(1) auxiliary storage; inputs unchanged, no omission\/shared state.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > const auto f=dm::stats::f_test_variance({1,2,3},{2,4,6});
-- > std::print("{:.2f}\n",f.estimate1); // 0.25
--
-- __References__
--
-- * https:\/\/stat.ethz.ch\/R-manual\/R-devel\/library\/stats\/html\/var.test.html
--
-- Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: @alternative@ = @AlternativeTwoSided@, @conf\_level@ = @0.95@.
--
-- C++: @HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::f\_test\_variance(const std::vector\<double\> & x, const std::vector\<double\> & y, Alternative alternative = TwoSided, double conf\_level = 0.94999999999999996)@
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.InvalidArgument') for C++ @std::invalid\_argument@: Too few\/nonfinite observations, nonpositive\/nonfinite variances or invalid controls.
--
-- See also: 'HypothesisTestResult'.
fTestVariance
  :: [P.Double]  -- ^ At least two finite first-sample values with positive finite sample variance.
  -> [P.Double]  -- ^ At least two finite independent second-sample values with positive finite sample variance.
  -> P.IO HypothesisTestResult  -- ^ statistic=estimate1=variance ratio, parameter1\/2=sample sizes minus one, p\_value and ratio confidence bounds; one-sided interval ends at zero or infinity.
fTestVariance _x1 _x2 = R.call 225 [R.toCpp _x1, R.toCpp _x2] P.>>= R.result

