  -> P.IO HypothesisTestResult  -- ^ statistic=positive rank sum V, estimate1=mu (not an estimated pseudomedian), approximate p\_value; no CI.
wilcoxonSignedRankTestWithAlternative _x1 _x2 _x3 = R.call 226 [R.toCpp _x1, R.toCpp _x2, R.toCpp _x3] P.>>= R.result

-- | Tie\/continuity-corrected Mann-Whitney U test using a normal approximation.
--
-- Pools ranks, giving average ranks to ties, and reports U for the first sample. Its identical- distribution null requires independent exchangeable samples. A common-shape shift model supports a location interpretation; equal medians alone is insufficient. U\/(n\_x\*n\_y) estimates P(X\>Y)+P(X=Y)\/2, not a mean difference. Approximation is used at every size. O(N log N) work\/O(N) storage, inputs unchanged, no shared state.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > const auto w=dm::stats::wilcoxon_rank_sum_test({1,2,3},{4,5,6});
-- > std::print("{:.0f}\n",w.statistic); // 0
--
-- __References__
--
-- * https:\/\/stat.ethz.ch\/R-manual\/R-devel\/library\/stats\/html\/wilcox.test.html
--
-- Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: @alternative@ = @AlternativeTwoSided@.
--
-- C++: @HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::wilcoxon\_rank\_sum\_test(const std::vector\<double\> & x, const std::vector\<double\> & y, Alternative alternative = TwoSided)@
--
-- Throws t'R.CppError' ('R.InvalidArgument') for C++ @std::invalid\_argument@: Empty\/nonfinite samples, invalid alternative or zero rank variance (all pooled observations tied).
--
-- See also: 'HypothesisTestResult'.
wilcoxonRankSumTest
  :: [P.Double]  -- ^ Nonempty finite first sample.
  -> [P.Double]  -- ^ Nonempty finite independent second sample.
  -> P.IO HypothesisTestResult  -- ^ statistic=U=rank\_sum(x)-n\_x(n\_x+1)\/2 and approximate p\_value; no CI or location estimate.
wilcoxonRankSumTest _x1 _x2 = R.call 227 [R.toCpp _x1, R.toCpp _x2] P.>>= R.result

-- | Tie\/continuity-corrected Mann-Whitney U test using a normal approximation.
--
-- Pools ranks, giving average ranks to ties, and reports U for the first sample. Its identical- distribution null requires independent exchangeable samples. A common-shape shift model supports a location interpretation; equal medians alone is insufficient. U\/(n\_x\*n\_y) estimates P(X\>Y)+P(X=Y)\/2, not a mean difference. Approximation is used at every size. O(N log N) work\/O(N) storage, inputs unchanged, no shared state.
--
-- __Example__
--
-- > const auto w=dm::stats::wilcoxon_rank_sum_test({1,2,3},{4,5,6});
